// =============================================================================
// Detection: Break-glass (emergency access) account sign-in — ANY sign-in
// Lab:       Contoso Health Identity Platform
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// WHAT IT CATCHES
//   ANY authentication — success OR failure, interactive OR non-interactive —
//   by either emergency-access account (bg-admin-01 / bg-admin-02). These
//   accounts hold standing Global Administrator, are cloud-only, sign in with
//   FIDO2 keys (a human step: lab/terraform/README.md, human step 6), and
//   are *excluded* from all Conditional Access and from PIM. They should be
//   used only when normal admin access is impossible. Therefore every sign-in
//   is high-signal and must be alerted on immediately (threshold = 1).
//
// TABLE / SOURCE
//   union of:
//     SigninLogs                        (interactive user sign-ins)
//     AADNonInteractiveUserSignInLogs   (token refresh / background / legacy)
//   Both from Microsoft Entra ID -> Diagnostic settings -> Log Analytics.
//   The union matters: an attacker using a stolen token or a legacy protocol
//   may never show in SigninLogs alone.
//
// LICENSE
//   - SigninLogs + AADNonInteractiveUserSignInLogs export requires Entra ID P1.
//   - Runs as a Log Analytics scheduled query or a Microsoft Sentinel rule.
//
// FALSE-POSITIVE NOTES
//   - The only "expected" hit is a planned break-glass drill. A quarterly
//     drill is designed, not yet run; the drill procedure is in
//     lab/docs/runbooks/break-glass-drill.md. Even then it should alert —
//     you WANT the record. Suppress by correlating with a pre-logged change
//     ticket, never by muting the rule.
//   - Matching is on the accounts' IMMUTABLE object ids (UserId), rendered by
//     modules/monitoring from modules/groups' break_glass_user_object_ids
//     into the BreakGlassUserIds placeholder below (2026-11-07, review
//     finding D3). A rename of bg-admin-01 therefore does NOT blind the rule.
//     The naming convention (bg-admin-NN@...) is kept as a second, OR-ed
//     match: it covers a NEW break-glass account before the next apply adds
//     its id, and it is what a paste of this file (empty id list) still
//     catches. Either match alone raises the alert.
//
// SENTINEL RULE: NEAR-REAL-TIME (NRT), since 2026-11-05
//   modules/monitoring deploys this query as an NRT rule: it runs every
//   minute over the rows INGESTED in the previous minute (two-minute built-in
//   delay), so neither the data source's delay nor a late run can drop a row
//   (Learn, "Quick threat detection with near-real-time (NRT) analytics
//   rules"). That is why the query has NO time filter: a TimeGenerated
//   window would drop a sign-in ingested late, and an ingestion_time()
//   window equal to a 5-minute schedule (the first fix, #170) could miss a
//   row when a run is late or skipped. Each ingested row is judged exactly
//   once, so one sign-in no longer raises 12 alerts (incidents #42 / #48).
//   Severity High · Entities: Account=UserPrincipalName + AadUserId=UserId,
//   IP=IPAddress.
//   The Log Analytics alert path (post-trial) runs the same query on its
//   own 1 h window.
//
//   DEDUP (2026-11-07, review finding D2): one sign-in writes several rows
//   (interactive + non-interactive legs, MFA steps) that are ingested over up
//   to ~30 minutes, and each NRT run that sees new rows raises one alert
//   (E49a: 12 alerts; E49c: three tickets for one sign-in). Now:
//     - incident grouping is by ACCOUNT only over 24 h (was account + IP over
//       1 h), so every late row and every new IP of the same break-glass
//       account lands in ONE incident;
//     - the notify playbook runs again when an alert joins, but it finds the
//       incident's open ServiceNow ticket (correlation_id = incident name)
//       and adds a work note to it; it posts NO second Teams card and opens
//       NO second ticket. Result: one incident, one card, one ticket per
//       account per day, with every joined alert noted on that ticket.
//   Guarded by modules/monitoring/tests/notify.tftest.hcl (NRT, no time
//   filter) and the Kusto harness case late-ingested-old-row.
//
// AUTOMATED RESPONSE (Logic App - lab/monitoring/playbook-notify.json)
//   Shared by all five rules, Sentinel path only (the post-trial Log
//   Analytics alerts just email the ag-identity-secops action group).
//   Incident created -> automation rule (lab/terraform/modules/monitoring) ->
//   (1) Teams adaptive card: incident severity, title, number, created time,
//       status, description and an "Open in Sentinel" link;
//   (2) ServiceNow incident: urgency/impact from severity (High -> urgency 1
//       / impact 1, P1 under ServiceNow's default priority matrix - verify:
//       on the PDI),
//       category "inquiry"; (3) the INC number is commented back on the
//       Sentinel incident for the audit trail.
//   Process, not automation: break-glass use is always reviewed by a second
//   admin per the runbook.
// =============================================================================

// Break-glass accounts by IMMUTABLE object id. Terraform renders the list
// (modules/monitoring, var.break_glass_user_ids); an empty list here means
// "no ids known" and the naming convention below still matches.
let BreakGlassUserIds = dynamic([]); // @@BREAK_GLASS_USER_IDS@@
// Break-glass naming convention from lab/terraform/modules/groups.
let BreakGlassPattern = @"(?i)^bg-admin-\d{2}@";
// SCHEMA TRAP: LocationDetails is `dynamic` in SigninLogs but `string` in
// AADNonInteractiveUserSignInLogs. A plain union splits it into
// LocationDetails_dynamic / LocationDetails_string and the property access
// below stops resolving (Sentinel rejects the rule). Normalise the
// non-interactive leg to dynamic first. (verify once in Log Analytics)
union
    (SigninLogs
        | extend SignInType = "interactive"),
    (AADNonInteractiveUserSignInLogs
        | extend SignInType = "non-interactive"
        | extend LocationDetails = todynamic(tostring(LocationDetails)))
| where UserId in (BreakGlassUserIds) or UserPrincipalName matches regex BreakGlassPattern
| extend MatchedBy = iff(UserId in (BreakGlassUserIds), "object id", "naming convention")
| extend Country = tostring(LocationDetails.countryOrRegion)
// One row PER ACCOUNT (the newest of its rows, with a count): the NRT rule
// makes one alert per row (AlertPerResult), so two break-glass accounts seen
// in the same minute become two alerts, each groupable into its own
// account's incident, instead of one alert naming both (review of #230).
// The newest row's fields stay in the plain columns; IPAddresses, ResultTypes
// and CorrelationIds keep EVERY sign-in of the account in the window, so an
// older success from another IP is still in the alert, not only in the raw
// logs (review of #230). Capped at 50 each (one NRT minute holds far fewer).
| summarize arg_max(TimeGenerated, SignInType, MatchedBy, IPAddress, Country, AppDisplayName,
                    ClientAppUsed, ResultType, ResultDescription, ConditionalAccessStatus, CorrelationId),
            SignIns = count(), SignInTypes = make_set(SignInType, 2),
            IPAddresses = make_set(IPAddress, 50), ResultTypes = make_set(tostring(ResultType), 50),
            CorrelationIds = make_set(CorrelationId, 50)
    by UserPrincipalName, UserId
| project TimeGenerated, SignInType, SignInTypes, SignIns, UserPrincipalName, UserId, MatchedBy, IPAddress, Country,
          AppDisplayName, ClientAppUsed, ResultType, ResultDescription,
          ConditionalAccessStatus, CorrelationId, IPAddresses, ResultTypes, CorrelationIds
| sort by TimeGenerated desc
